      "text": "Eager vs Lazy loading",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Eager vs Lazy loading",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "EQa-FgSXn3MQONH07jf1s",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 467.5997009277344,
      "y": 2248.5000076293945,
      "width": 244.79998779296875,
      "height": 64.79998779296875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0I",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 1458710101,
      "version": 44,
      "versionNonce": 969300117,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779476634310,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "6WQv2nZSQMb4OWwuCOfus",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 292.5997009277344,
      "y": 2349.900001525879,
      "width": 1049.0592041015625,
      "height": 5450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 222466645,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 587710741,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779476878280,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In Hibernate/JPA, Lazy Loading and Eager Loading define when related data is fetched from the database.\n\nThink of it like:\n\n“Should Hibernate load related objects immediately, or only when actually needed?”\n\nExample\n\nSuppose:\n\nclass User {\n    List<Order> orders;\n}\n\nA user can have many orders.\n\nNow the question is:\n\nWhen we fetch User, should orders also be fetched immediately?\n\nThat’s where lazy/eager loading comes in.\n\n1. Lazy Loading\nMeaning\n\nRelated data is loaded only when accessed.\n\nExample:\n\nUser user = userRepo.findById(1).get();\n\nOnly User data is fetched initially.\n\nOrders are NOT fetched yet.\n\nOnly when:\n\nuser.getOrders();\n\nHibernate fires another SQL query.\n\nAnnotation\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\nprivate List<Order> orders;\nSQL Behavior\nFirst query\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1;\nLater when accessing orders\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 1;\nAdvantages\nBetter performance initially\n\nLess data fetched.\n\nSaves memory\n\nUseful for large relationships.\n\nRecommended default\n\nEspecially for:\n\n@OneToMany\n@ManyToMany\nDisadvantages\nCan cause LazyInitializationException\n\nExample:\n\nUser user = service.getUser();\nsession.close();\n\nuser.getOrders(); // ERROR\n\nBecause Hibernate session is closed.\n\n2. Eager Loading\nMeaning\n\nRelated data is fetched immediately with parent entity.\n\nExample:\n\nUser user = userRepo.findById(1).get();\n\nUser AND orders are loaded together.\n\nAnnotation\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)\nprivate List<Order> orders;\nSQL Behavior\n\nHibernate may generate:\n\nSELECT *\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o\nON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE u.id = 1;\n\nor multiple immediate queries.\n\nAdvantages\nSimpler\n\nNo LazyInitializationException.\n\nConvenient\n\nData available immediately.\n\nDisadvantages\nPerformance issues\n\nSuppose:\n\nUser has 10,000 orders\n\nEvery user fetch loads all orders even if unused.\n\nCan create:\n\nhuge joins\nmemory waste\nslower APIs\nDefault Fetch Types in JPA\nRelationship        Default\n@OneToMany        LAZY\n@ManyToMany        LAZY\n@ManyToOne        EAGER\n@OneToOne        EAGER\nReal-world Analogy\nLazy Loading\n\nNetflix loads movie details only when you click the movie.\n\nGood for performance.\n\nEager Loading\n\nOpening Amazon app and loading:\n\nall products\nall reviews\nall seller info\nall recommendations\n\nimmediately.\n\nSlow and wasteful.\n\nBest Practice\n\nIn real backend systems:\n\n✅ Prefer LAZY loading by default.\n\nThen explicitly fetch data when needed using:\n\nJOIN FETCH\nDTO projections\nEntity graphs\nExample Best Practice\n\nInstead of EAGER:\n\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\nprivate List<Order> orders;\n\nUse query:\n\n@Query(\"\"\"\nSELECT u FROM User u\nJOIN FETCH u.orders\nWHERE u.id = :id\n\"\"\")\nUser findUserWithOrders(Long id);\n\nThis gives:\n\ncontrolled loading\nbetter performance\navoids N+1 problems\nN+1 Query Problem\n\nVery important Hibernate issue.\n\nExample:\n\nList<User> users = userRepo.findAll();\n\nThen:\n\nfor(User u : users) {\n    u.getOrders();\n}\n\nResults:\n\n1 query for users\nN queries for orders\n\nTotal:\n\n1 + N queries\n\nBad for performance.\n\nSummary\nFeature        Lazy        Eager\nWhen data loads        On demand        Immediately\nPerformance        Better usually        Can be worse\nMemory usage        Lower        Higher\nRisk        LazyInitializationException        Over-fetching\nRecommended        Usually yes        Use carefully\nRule followed by experienced backend engineers\n\n“Make everything LAZY unless there is a strong reason not to.”",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In Hibernate/JPA, Lazy Loading and Eager Loading define when related data is fetched from the database.\n\nThink of it like:\n\n“Should Hibernate load related objects immediately, or only when actually needed?”\n\nExample\n\nSuppose:\n\nclass User {\n    List<Order> orders;\n}\n\nA user can have many orders.\n\nNow the question is:\n\nWhen we fetch User, should orders also be fetched immediately?\n\nThat’s where lazy/eager loading comes in.\n\n1. Lazy Loading\nMeaning\n\nRelated data is loaded only when accessed.\n\nExample:\n\nUser user = userRepo.findById(1).get();\n\nOnly User data is fetched initially.\n\nOrders are NOT fetched yet.\n\nOnly when:\n\nuser.getOrders();\n\nHibernate fires another SQL query.\n\nAnnotation\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\nprivate List<Order> orders;\nSQL Behavior\nFirst query\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1;\nLater when accessing orders\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 1;\nAdvantages\nBetter performance initially\n\nLess data fetched.\n\nSaves memory\n\nUseful for large relationships.\n\nRecommended default\n\nEspecially for:\n\n@OneToMany\n@ManyToMany\nDisadvantages\nCan cause LazyInitializationException\n\nExample:\n\nUser user = service.getUser();\nsession.close();\n\nuser.getOrders(); // ERROR\n\nBecause Hibernate session is closed.\n\n2. Eager Loading\nMeaning\n\nRelated data is fetched immediately with parent entity.\n\nExample:\n\nUser user = userRepo.findById(1).get();\n\nUser AND orders are loaded together.\n\nAnnotation\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)\nprivate List<Order> orders;\nSQL Behavior\n\nHibernate may generate:\n\nSELECT *\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o\nON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE u.id = 1;\n\nor multiple immediate queries.\n\nAdvantages\nSimpler\n\nNo LazyInitializationException.\n\nConvenient\n\nData available immediately.\n\nDisadvantages\nPerformance issues\n\nSuppose:\n\nUser has 10,000 orders\n\nEvery user fetch loads all orders even if unused.\n\nCan create:\n\nhuge joins\nmemory waste\nslower APIs\nDefault Fetch Types in JPA\nRelationship        Default\n@OneToMany        LAZY\n@ManyToMany        LAZY\n@ManyToOne        EAGER\n@OneToOne        EAGER\nReal-world Analogy\nLazy Loading\n\nNetflix loads movie details only when you click the movie.\n\nGood for performance.\n\nEager Loading\n\nOpening Amazon app and loading:\n\nall products\nall reviews\nall seller info\nall recommendations\n\nimmediately.\n\nSlow and wasteful.\n\nBest Practice\n\nIn real backend systems:\n\n✅ Prefer LAZY loading by default.\n\nThen explicitly fetch data when needed using:\n\nJOIN FETCH\nDTO projections\nEntity graphs\nExample Best Practice\n\nInstead of EAGER:\n\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\nprivate List<Order> orders;\n\nUse query:\n\n@Query(\"\"\"\nSELECT u FROM User u\nJOIN FETCH u.orders\nWHERE u.id = :id\n\"\"\")\nUser findUserWithOrders(Long id);\n\nThis gives:\n\ncontrolled loading\nbetter performance\navoids N+1 problems\nN+1 Query Problem\n\nVery important Hibernate issue.\n\nExample:\n\nList<User> users = userRepo.findAll();\n\nThen:\n\nfor(User u : users) {\n    u.getOrders();\n}\n\nResults:\n\n1 query for users\nN queries for orders\n\nTotal:\n\n1 + N queries\n\nBad for performance.\n\nSummary\nFeature        Lazy        Eager\nWhen data loads        On demand        Immediately\nPerformance        Better usually        Can be worse\nMemory usage        Lower        Higher\nRisk        LazyInitializationException        Over-fetching\nRecommended        Usually yes        Use carefully\nRule followed by experienced backend engineers\n\n“Make everything LAZY unless there is a strong reason not to.”",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
